  "body": "## Change and mathematical scope\n\nThis adds only `research/ut4-two-target-r2/`. It is an exhaustive finite negative result in the explicit `F2[UT4(F2)]` alphabet of https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1662. No new \u03ba, no supplier, and no change to the selected record, `upstream/` or any frozen pin.\n\n**Result.** With r = w = 2 (no extra roles), no word of 14 paid factors `I+([g]+1)xy^T` (g in {a,b,c}) equals the two-target endpoint `I+([z]+1)I_2`. No such word reaches even the degree-three quotient endpoint. This answers CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1662's open question for two roles.\n\n**Every-r corollary, conditional.** @ToikY's concurrent https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1680 (head `a281330e`) proves in writing that every 14/15-call two-target word with any r \u2265 2 compresses to two roles. Together: two central targets need **at least 15 calls for every finite r**, so the interval is 15..16. That corollary rests on CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1680's written lemma, which this package does not re-derive.\n\n## Method\n\nCrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1662's written bound forces counts (4,6,4). The search then:\n\n1. Enumerates the zero-sum six-call middle sequences up to GL2(F2) conjugation. The 5,582 representatives' orbits are checked to cover all 33,489.\n2. Reduces each a/c placement to its prefix-sum profile at the six middle calls (20,791 profiles), screened by the a/b-only and c/b-only quotient coefficients.\n3. Pairs profiles through the vectors `u_j = P_j x_j`, `w_j = P_j\u1d40 y_j`, on which `abc` and `cba` depend.\n4. Expands every surviving pair into all concrete words. Each word is checked against the full quotient and, separately, the full formal group-algebra endpoint.\n\n| Stage | Count |\n|---|---:|\n| Canonical middle sequences | 5,582 |\n| Key pairs / profile pairs with `abc = cba = I` | 184 / 208 |\n| Concrete words | 3,248 |\n| Pass quotient endpoint / formal endpoint | 0 / 0 |\n\n## Evidence\n\n`python3.12 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/ut4-two-target-r2` gives `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE research/ut4-two-target-r2 outcome=negative-result`. Local run: CPython 3.12 on Windows x86_64, about 4 min (`verify.py` 227 s, then 14 tests in 12 s). `--check-only` also passes.\n\n- `verify.py` independently checks:\n  - that `g \u21a6 1+g` is multiplicative from UT4(F2) into the quotient on all 4,096 pairs, with `z \u21a6 1+abc+acb+bac+cba`;\n  - GL2 orbit coverage;\n  - CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1662's direct 16-call word as a positive control, with all 16 one-call omissions failing;\n  - every stage count of the full search.\n- `test_package.py` (14 tests):\n  - the profile and key formulas against direct quotient products on random words;\n  - indexed key pairing against brute force;\n  - that the formal product is in word order;\n  - planted-word completeness: random (4,6,4) words are regenerated by the enumeration;\n  - a slow reference driver that finds `(abcb)^2` at r = 1 and nothing for counts (2,3,2).\n- Six source mutations (operand order, the `bab` sum, cross order, quotient reduction, left versus right multiplication, the central target) are each killed by the tests. An earlier run showed the multiplication-order mutant surviving; the order test was added to catch it.\n\n**Not checked:**\n- CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1662's count lower bound and CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1680's compression lemma are cited, not re-derived.\n- No proof-assistant or independent human review.\n- No physical, compiler, hosting, precision or analytic obligations; no selected cold replay (nothing in the selected chain changes).\n- On this Windows machine, 6 of the 20 `tests/test_research_*.py` workflow tests error inside their own temporary repositories (`source has uncommitted changes ... input.txt`, a line-ending issue). The other 14 pass. The errors are unrelated to this package.\n\n## Task and overlap\n\n- Card `bit-new-register-model`, priority revision 3, chooser nonce `f9a363c919f7441f52bd6dbdade3397d`, scope key `7084092dd726e7ba1f2f`. Scope is **refined** to CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1662's open next question with r = w = 2; see `TASK.json`.\n- Base main `a18b4b0136d647529971e9fcb99fa83f5b9a0b81`.\n- Claim: https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1647#discussioncomment-18916137.\n- The final overlap refresh (07:06 UTC, 383 open PRs and 333 discussion records, complete pagination) found only CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1662 and the complementary CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1680, which cites this claim.\n\n## Open next questions\n\n1. Is 15 attainable at r = 2? By CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1680 that decides the exact cost for every r. The first step is to run the pipeline on the splits (5,6,4), (4,7,4) and (4,6,5).\n2. An independent check of CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds#1680's compression lemma.\n\n## Credit\n\n@ToikY (https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1662 for the model, bound and question; https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1680 for the workspace compression). Also steph4n-gh (https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1573), Arham Tahir (https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1589) and Hanson Sun / @bbMisk (https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1607). No predecessor code is imported.\n\nPrepared by Aaron Wang / navinor, with help from Claude Code. Apache-2.0. Not requesting merge; the selected frontier is unchanged.\n",
